        "id": "routes-and-merchants",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Networks across land and sea",
        "content": "Iran’s early modern commerce operated through overlapping land and maritime networks. Under the Safavids, the dynasty that ruled from the beginning of the sixteenth century, merchants traded with Ottoman territories, India, Russia, and Central Asia. European trading companies added another layer to these relationships. Maritime commerce through the Persian Gulf expanded substantially, but overland exchange continued: one route did not simply replace another. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vi/)\n\nSilk was a prominent export, alongside products including horses, goat hair, and pearls. Imports included textiles, spices, metals, and sugar. Armenian merchants became important participants in the silk business, connecting production with markets abroad. Commerce also depended on arrangements with rulers, customs officials, brokers, and shipping companies. Shah Abbas I’s silk-export monopoly illustrates how a government could intervene directly in a valuable commodity rather than merely tax its movement. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vi/)\n\nThe surviving evidence is uneven. European company archives illuminate particular transactions, but their share of total Iranian commerce is uncertain. They cannot establish a complete national trade balance for the early modern period. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vi/)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
