      {
        "id": "regulation-and-centralization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From merchant commerce to administrative control",
        "content": "Before the Pahlavi monarchy, foreign merchants operated under treaty protections, while Iranian merchants also served as agents for overseas firms. These overlapping roles make a simple division between domestic and foreign commercial activity misleading. During the Pahlavi period, government intervention expanded through import restrictions, commodity quotas, and state trading companies. Export certificates linked permission to import with prior export activity, making administrative access part of a merchant’s business. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vii/)\n\nCommercial decision-making also became more concentrated in Tehran. Transport connections, government offices, and state monopoly companies drew activity toward the capital, reducing the relative autonomy of provincial trading centers. This geographical shift helps explain why the history of foreign trade belongs to the history of Iranian cities as well as international relations. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vii/)\n\nAfter the revolution and during the war with Iraq, administrative controls sharply restricted private importing. Nowshirvani’s historical account distinguishes constitutional provisions and proposed legislation from the administrative measures actually used. It describes the expansion of government and foundation participation; it should not be read as a description of today’s legal arrangements. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/commerce-vii/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
