    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s social history examines how people organized households, livelihoods, settlements, and access to learning across changing political and economic conditions. It connects urban, rural, and nomadic experiences while recognizing substantial differences between regions, social groups, and generations.",
    "tags": ["Social history", "Rural life", "Urbanization", "Nomadism", "Families", "Education", "Migration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T15:18:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T15:18:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Social History: Families, Settlements and Learning | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the connected histories of Iranian villages, cities, pastoral communities, families, education, and internal migration."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "social-history-perspective",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "History at the scale of everyday life",
        "content": "A change of dynasty does not tell us, by itself, how a household earned a living, who controlled a harvest, or how children learned to read. Social history follows these relationships across political turning points. In Iran, it must also connect distinct settings: villages organized around cultivation, mobile pastoral communities using seasonal grazing, and households whose responsibilities changed alongside wage employment and public services. These settings were interconnected rather than successive stages through which everyone passed. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/deh/) [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/nomadism/) [s3](https://www.persee.fr/doc/pop_1634-2941_2002_num_57_2_18397)",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-settlement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Villages were not all alike",
        "content": "The Persian term deh denotes a village understood as a social and spatial unit. Its physical form varied with ecology and local history. On arid foothill plains, clustered settlements often developed around irrigation channels or qanats, underground galleries that convey groundwater. Water access could reflect differences in wealth and influence within the settlement. In the humid Caspian provinces of Gilan and Mazandaran, dispersed farmsteads created a markedly different landscape. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/deh/)\n\nSettlement patterns could also preserve histories of mobility. Some communities originating in pastoral groups combined a permanent village with temporary camps or complementary agricultural land. A house, therefore, does not prove that seasonal movement had ended. This regional variety makes any single picture of the timeless Iranian village inadequate. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/deh/)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "land-and-power",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Landownership was a social relationship",
        "content": "Amlak, privately owned agricultural estates, could encompass several villages. Their administration linked landowners, estate managers, village headmen, and cultivators. Sharecropping distributed agricultural output according to contributions that could include land, water, seed, animals, and labor. Ownership consequently affected authority and dependence as well as income. [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/amlak/)\n\nAbsentee ownership further complicates a sharp division between countryside and city: an estate owner might live in town while a manager supervised production. The land reform implemented during the Pahlavi period challenged this system of large estates. Its significance was social as well as agricultural, because it altered established relationships between cultivators and those who controlled village resources. The history of rural life therefore requires attention to power and unequal access, alongside crops and architecture. [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/amlak/)",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "pastoral-mobility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mobility as an organized livelihood",
        "content": "Pastoral nomadism involved regular movement along known routes between grazing areas. In Iran’s mountain environments, summer uplands and winter lowlands offered complementary resources. Such movement was an organized response to seasonality, not aimless wandering. Herds linked households to landscapes unsuitable for continuous cultivation. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/nomadism/)\n\nGovernment settlement policies, pressure on grazing areas, schooling, and integration into wider markets changed pastoral life. These developments did not produce one uniform outcome: continued migration, partial settlement, and changing combinations of occupations all belong to the history. It is therefore essential to distinguish tribal affiliation, which concerns social identity and organization, from the actual practice of moving livestock and households. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/nomadism/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "family-functions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Households, kinship, and public services",
        "content": "Historian and demographer Marie Ladier-Fouladi describes the family as historically providing much more than domestic companionship: kin could help secure work, arrange marriage, support education, and care for older relatives. Her study connects changes in these functions with wage labor, urbanization, and access to state-funded services. This is an interpretation of social transformation, rather than proof that extended kinship simply disappeared. [s3](https://www.persee.fr/doc/pop_1634-2941_2002_num_57_2_18397)\n\nThe distinction between a family and a household matters. A census household records a residential arrangement; relationships of support can extend beyond it. Ladier-Fouladi also identifies limitations in older census categories and fragmentary surveys. A smaller household should therefore not automatically be interpreted as weaker family ties or greater personal autonomy. [s3](https://www.persee.fr/doc/pop_1634-2941_2002_num_57_2_18397)",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "learning-and-uneven-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning and the uneven reach of change",
        "content": "Education connects these histories because access to learning depends on where people live, their work, and their family circumstances. UNESCO’s account of a literacy and computer-skills initiative recognized in 2018 describes the Literacy Movement Organization’s attention to women and girls in rural areas and other underserved groups. It documents a particular program and its stated priorities; an award does not establish that educational inequality was eliminated. [s5](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/unesco-literacy-prize-awarded-project-iran-promoting-literacy-and-computer-skills)\n\nThe branches below pursue these distinctions separately. Urbanization concerns the changing distribution and organization of settlement; migration concerns movement between places; family history examines relationships that may persist across both. Read together with rural and nomadic histories, they show why social change should be investigated through specific institutions and experiences rather than described as a single national journey from tradition to modernity. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/deh/) [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/nomadism/) [s3](https://www.persee.fr/doc/pop_1634-2941_2002_num_57_2_18397)",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
